  <h2 id="reliquary-heading">The Reliquary</h2>

  <%- include("../partials/location-image", {
    src: "/images/the-reliquary-v2.webp",
    caption: "A place for what survives of the written record.",
  }) %>

  <p>The Reliquary keeps what survives of Drevan’s histories. Read the accounts side by side and the gaps begin to show.</p>

  <article aria-labelledby="drevan-heading">
    <h3 id="drevan-heading">The world of Drevan</h3>
    <p>Before the fall, Drevan’s civilizations learned to work magic and machinery together. Great engines drew on energies their descendants scarcely understand, and whole cities lived beneath artificial stars. Today, scattered settlements make what they can of the structures and machines those civilizations left behind. Few agree on what ended the old world.</p>

    <h4>Nexaris</h4>
    <p>Nexaris grew among the remains of the old world. Old machinery stands beside modest homes. There is room here for wanderers, merchants, fighters, and anyone looking to begin again.</p>
    <p>Some earn their gold in <a href="/game/arena">Dominion</a> and spend it patching themselves up at <a href="/game/hospital">Sanctum</a>. Others fish at <a href="/game/fishing">The Driftwater</a> or trade at <a href="/game/store">Curious Emporium</a>. How you make a living here is up to you.</p>
    <p><em>The old world is gone. The unknown awaits.</em></p>
  </article>

  <article aria-labelledby="absolute-heading">
    <h3 id="absolute-heading">The Absolute</h3>
    <p><em>The Last Truth</em></p>
    <p>This surviving account traces the fall to a question the scholars of the old world could never leave alone.</p>

    <h4>The Age Before</h4>
    <p>In the age of artificial stars, machines bent the laws of nature and scholars drew energy from distant dimensions. Their work eventually led them to ask what had existed before creation.</p>

    <h4>The Axiom Engine</h4>
    <p>Deep beneath Drevan, the scholars built the Axiom Engine to search beyond the known universe for the principles on which reality rested. Generations tended the machine through centuries of silence. Then it answered.</p>

    <h4>The Last Truth</h4>
    <p>According to the account, the Engine reached an intelligence beyond reality, without beginning or end. The scholars could neither measure nor contain it. They called it The Absolute.</p>
    <p>The account gives the intelligence no malice. It understood existence as an error.</p>

    <h4>The Great Unmaking</h4>
    <p>The scholars tried to sever the connection, but cities were already vanishing without a trace of destruction. Monuments still stood for people no one could remember. Roads led to cities that had never been built. Records contradicted the memories of the people who wrote them.</p>
    <p>Machines went on working after the names of their makers vanished. As the collapse spread, neither weapons nor sorcery could restore what had been unmade.</p>

    <h4>The Final Seal</h4>
    <p>The surviving scholars built magical barriers and mechanical seals around the Engine. They laid stone, metal, and machinery in layers between it and the surface. The unmaking stopped. The record ends without saying what became of the scholars.</p>

    <h4>The Ruins</h4>
    <p>Thousands of years passed. Settlements rose among the remnants, and the structures around the buried Engine came to be called <a href="/game/ruins">The Ruins</a>. Explorers still search the upper chambers for valuables and working relics.</p>
    <p>Few return from the deeper levels. Those who do speak of impossible architecture, machines with no apparent source of power, and voices in languages no living scholar can identify. Some describe the deeper chambers as a prison.</p>

    <h4>What Remains</h4>
    <p>The account holds that the Engine still runs beneath the deepest chambers and that its connection endures. Whatever the scholars reached may still be pursuing the same purpose.</p>
    <p>Some scholars blame The Absolute for the fall. Others believe it was trying to correct something the builders misunderstood. A few surviving fragments suggest the builders may have mistaken what they reached for the greatest danger beyond creation.</p>
  </article>
</section>
